script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"History: To understand the glorious history behind this saffron-colored image of Shri Ajahara Parshwanath, one must know the story of the illustrious Suryavanshi (Sun) dynasty of Ayodhya, which included such mighty kings as Ikshvaku, Purandar, Kirtidhar, Sukoshal, Hiranyagarbh, Nadhush, Kakutstha, Harishchandra, Mandhata, Sagar, Bhagirath, Ambarish, Dilip, Raghu, Dasharath, and Ram. The 25th king of this Suryavanshi line was Ajaypal. When Ajaypal became king, he made Saketpur his capital. King Ajaypal, who was a Jain, once went on pilgrimage to the sacred Jain tirtha of Shatrunjaya. Upon reaching the port of Diu, he fell ill with various ailments, and so encamped there for a long time with his army. Around this time, a merchant named Ratnasar was engaged in overseas trade with his ships, when his vessels were caught in a terrible storm in the middle of the ocean. Ratnasar prayed to God, whereupon a divine voice told him that in the depths of the sea lay a chest made of kalpavriksha (wish-fulfilling tree) wood, containing a very ancient and powerful image of Lord Parshwanath — one that had been worshipped for 7 lakh years by the deity Dharanendra, for 600 years by the deity Kubera, and for 7 lakh years by the deity Varuna. The goddess Padmavati told Ratnasar to give this image to King Ajaypal, who was then encamped at the port of Diu. Ratnasar retrieved the image from the sea and, with a great procession, presented it to King Ajaypal.\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1d:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"article\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"0\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"56th of the 108 Parshwanaths - Shri Ajahara Parshwanath\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"1\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Main Deity: The image of Lord Shri Ajahara Parshwanath, deep saffron in color, seated in the lotus (padmasana) posture, is 43 centimeters high. The image is made of sand, and above its head is a canopy of 7 serpent hoods.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"2\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Location: This temple is on the outskirts of the village of Anjar.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"3\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"$23\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"4\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"King Ajaypal held a grand snatra (ceremonial bathing) festival for the image and applied the sanctified water to his body, and within a few days he was cured of all his ailments. Deeply moved by the miraculous power of this image, Ajaypal founded a new city and named it Ajaynagar. He built a beautiful and magnificent temple in the middle of the city and formally installed this miraculous image of Lord Parshwanath in it. King Ajaypal performed worship three times daily (trikal puja) and grew greatly prosperous and glorious; after residing in Ajaynagar for six months, he set out on pilgrimage to the sacred maha-tirtha of Shatrunjaya.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"5\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Since King Ajaypal had been cured of all his ailments (ajar), this Parshwanath came to be known as \\\\\\\"Ajahara Parshwanath.\\\\\\\" Even today, many illnesses of devotees are cured through the pakshal (ceremonial bathing water) of the image. The water used in the pakshal of the image is regarded as nectar by Jains and non-Jains alike. A bell found here bears an inscription from the year 1034. Several stone inscriptions from the 14th century of the Vikram era are found here, attesting to its antiquity. Several images in kayotsarga posture were consecrated in the temple in Vikram Samvat 1323 by the auspicious hands of Acharya Shri Mahendrasurishwarji. The image of Chintamani Parshwanath was consecrated in Vikram Samvat 1343. The 14th renovation of this temple was carried out in Vikram Samvat 1667 by Kunvarji Jivraj, a Shrimali resident of Una, in the presence of the Tapagachh Acharya Shri Vijaydevsuriji. Every year a fair is held here on Chaitra Purnima, Kartik Purnima, and the 10th day of the dark half of Paush.\\\"}],\\\"$L24\\\",\\\"$L25\\\",\\\"$L26\\\",\\\"$L27\\\",\\\"$L28\\\",\\\"$L29\\\",\\\"$L2a\\\",\\\"$L2b\\\"]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1e:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"nav\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L5\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-074\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":[[\\\"path\\\",{\\\"d\\\":\\\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\\\",\\\"key\\\":\\\"1wnfg3\\\"}]],\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Shri Navkhanda Parshwanath\\\"}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L5\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-076\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Shri Dokadiya Parshwanath\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":[[\\\"path\\\",{\\\"d\\\":\\\"m9 18 6-6-6-6\\\",\\\"key\\\":\\\"mthhwq\\\"}]],\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}]]}]]}]\\n1f:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"footer\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-auto border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 py-8\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 font-ui text-xs text-ink-soft flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-2 sm:items-center sm:justify-between\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"children\\\":\\\"१०८ पार्श्वनाथ तीर्थ दर्शन — श्री पार्श्वनाथ भगवान के तीर्थों का डिजिटल संकलन\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Original source: Rajendra Kumar Jain, Parshv Sadan, Parshv Nagar, Dungarpur\\\"}]]}]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"24:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"6\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Shri Jinprabhasuriji gave this Parshwanath the name \\\\\\\"Navnidhi Parshwanath.\\\\\\\" Many miracles have occurred here — even today, visitors sometimes see the roughly 6-foot form of the serpent-king (Dharanendra deva) worshipping before the image of Lord Parshwanath. This \\\\\\\"Ajahara Tirth\\\\\\\" is known as the \\\\\\\"Shankheshwar Tirth of Saurashtra.\\\\\\\"\\\"}]\\n25:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"7\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Other Temples: At present there are no other temples.\\\"}]\\n26:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"8\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Art and Sculpture: This is a very ancient temple that has been rebuilt as many as 14 times. There are more than 100 ancient stepwells here. Excavations in the surrounding area have uncovered many remains and carved stones bearing inscriptions, which prove the antiquity of the temple. There is a basement near the temple from which many images have been recovered. The main image is bright and striking to behold; it is made of sand and is saffron-colored.\\\"}]\\n27:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"9\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Directions: The nearest railway stations to the temple are Una, 5 kilometers away, and Delwada, 2.5 kilometers away. Una and Delwada are connected by good roads. Dharmashala and bhojanshala facilities are available here. The port, beach, and fort of Diu are 15 kilometers away.\\\"}]\\n28:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"10\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Scripture References: Shri Ajahara Parshwanath is mentioned in the \\\\\\\"Vividh Tirth Kalp,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"Nabhinandanadhar Pravad,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"Tirthamala,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"Dharmabuddhai,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"Sanghapati Samarsinh Ras,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"365 Shri Parshwa Jin Nammala,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"Shri Bhatecha Parshwanath Stavan,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"108 Namgarbhit Shri Godiji Parshwanath Stavan,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"108 Nam Garbhit Shri Parshwanath Stavan,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"Shri Parshwanath Sankhya Stavan,\\\\\\\" and others. In the 17th century, Shri Samsundarji wrote a bhas (devotional composition) on Shri Ajahara Parshwanath. An image of Shri Ajahara Parshwanath is also found at Jiravala Tirth and in the Kalikund Parshwanath temple in Santacruz, Mumbai.\\\"}]\\n29:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"11\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Praise (Stuti): He whose sacred bathing water removes all the ailments and sorrows of kings — O remover of fear, bestower of the bliss of liberation, known everywhere by the name Ajahara, worshipped by Dharanendra and other great celestial beings. With devotion I bow to Shri Ajahara Parshwa.\\\"}]\\n2a:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"12\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Chanting Mantra: \\\\\\\"Om Hrim Arham Shri Ajahara Parshwanathaya Namah.\\\\\\\"\\\"}]\\n2b:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"13\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Trust: Shri Ajahara Parshwanath Panchtirthi Jain Karkhana Pedhi, Village: Ajahara, Post: Delwada-362510, Taluka: Una, District: Junagadh, State of Gujarat, India. Phone: 02875-221628. Head Office: Una-362560, Phone: 02875-222233, Mobile: 9574869355.\\\"}]\\n\"])</script></body></html>","rscData":"","segmentData":{"/_tree":"","/_full":"","/$d$lang/tirtha/$d$slug/__PAGE__":"","/$d$lang/tirtha/$d$slug":"","/$d$lang/tirtha":"","/$d$lang":"","/_index":"","/_head":""},"headers":{"x-nextjs-stale-time":"300","x-nextjs-prerender":"1","x-next-cache-tags":"_N_T_/layout,_N_T_/[lang]/layout,_N_T_/[lang]/tirtha/layout,_N_T_/[lang]/tirtha/[slug]/layout,_N_T_/[lang]/tirtha/[slug]/page,_N_T_/en/tirtha/tirtha-075"},"segmentPaths":["/_tree","/_full","/$d$lang/tirtha/$d$slug/__PAGE__","/$d$lang/tirtha/$d$slug","/$d$lang/tirtha","/$d$lang","/_index","/_head"],"revalidate":60,"cacheControl":{